><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250509104032+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250509104035+10'00'" name="72e294df-a20d-40d3-863d-a97826082907" page="7" coords="46.602800,131.190000,88.079900,131.190000,46.602800,121.314000,88.079900,121.314000" rect="46.294200,121.006000,88.388500,131.498000" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study has introduced multiple new factors at broad&#xD;socioecological levels that should be considered alongside previously&#xD;identified intrapersonal and interpersonal level athlete attrition factors.&#xD;Athlete health, at the individual and system level, was considered the&#xD;most important and feasible athlete retention issue to address. Our results&#xD;support the use of a system approach to minimising athlete&#xD;attrition and improving the efficiency and health of high-performance&#xD;pathway systems.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="7" rect="595.276000,39.189500,779.276000,131.190000"
/></highlight
